00:32:22.060 --> 00:32:23.780
So I think that's one element,

00:32:23.780 --> 00:32:27.550
but I'm going to be starting with
Simon and we'll move back across.

00:32:27.550 --> 00:32:28.240
Can you say,

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:31.670
what are the other differentiating
features of a data driven firm.

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:35.508
&gt;&gt; So, I think LinkedIn, actually,

00:32:35.508 --> 00:32:40.890
these always start with
saying huge problem you know?

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:46.300
And the challenge was, about a three and
a half years ago I joined Linkedin and

00:32:46.300 --> 00:32:51.080
I support only one vertical
business at a moment.

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:53.509
So the first year, personally speaking,

00:32:53.509 --> 00:32:57.840
as a data scientist I close
500 different requests.

00:32:57.840 --> 00:33:02.630
Building models, visualization,
convincing stats that make decisions,

00:33:02.630 --> 00:33:04.442
support in PM marketers and sales people.

00:33:04.442 --> 00:33:07.300
So 500 different types of projects,

00:33:07.300 --> 00:33:11.000
I was really working around
the clock at that moment.

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:16.750
But at that moment I supported
200 people almost directly,

00:33:16.750 --> 00:33:18.620
if you could do the simple math, right?

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:22.612
Every year, every year,
500 divided by 200,

00:33:22.612 --> 00:33:27.310
I only answer two major questions
per employee every year.

00:33:27.310 --> 00:33:30.640
That's not data driven,
that's not inspiring.

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:36.300
So the problem is the system,
the data set is so complex and slow.

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:39.540
We found out not many people were able to

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:42.380
access those insightful
information to make decisions.

00:33:42.380 --> 00:33:48.370
Because they have to wait hours,
days, weeks to get the insights.

00:33:48.370 --> 00:33:49.450
How to do that?

00:33:49.450 --> 00:33:54.430
I ask all my internal clients quickly,
hey, what do you need?

00:33:54.430 --> 00:33:58.590
They say, Simon, very simple,
we need to have the insights very quickly.

00:33:59.620 --> 00:34:01.390
I say, how quickly?

00:34:01.390 --> 00:34:02.960
They ask, within two seconds.

00:34:05.070 --> 00:34:08.200
This is real,
this was [LAUGH] real, two seconds.

00:34:08.200 --> 00:34:11.560
I say, two seconds that's impossible,

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:14.350
I never see any system that can
answer questions like that.

00:34:14.350 --> 00:34:19.860
They said, who cares,
we only need this within two seconds.

00:34:19.860 --> 00:34:22.795
Longer than that, thank you very much,
you don't need to talk to me anymore.

00:34:22.795 --> 00:34:26.730
[LAUGH] And so
that's a huge challenge, right?

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:31.000
Before, it was hours, days, weeks,
and then we had to very innovative and

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:33.610
think of a way how to
correct that problem.

00:34:33.610 --> 00:34:36.440
Actually, as of today,
we developed a system.

00:34:36.440 --> 00:34:39.280
Actually, within three months after
that question had been raised.

00:34:39.280 --> 00:34:45.460
The response time, I would say recently,
is maybe a couple of hundred milliseconds.

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:48.930
When people click the button
they get exactly what they need.

00:34:48.930 --> 00:34:50.110
It's not what they ask,

00:34:50.110 --> 00:34:54.150
it's very different than comparison,
what's been asked and what they need.

00:34:54.150 --> 00:34:57.890
Then they can use the insider
to close the arsenal right away.

00:34:57.890 --> 00:35:02.172
So after that long we see daily adoptions,

00:35:02.172 --> 00:35:08.210
almost 90% employees for that particular
department, which is huge by the way.

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:12.410
So think about that, every week,
every day the are being used.

00:35:12.410 --> 00:35:16.920
And we see the hyper leap of revenue
growth, plus better user experience for

00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:19.030
LinkedIn members, like all of us.

00:35:19.030 --> 00:35:22.810
And I didn't inspire,
I think that's a key.

00:35:22.810 --> 00:35:25.820
How to make the incentive for
a lot of people and

00:35:25.820 --> 00:35:28.210
then help them to have a better life.

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:29.050
So, yeah.

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:36.830
&gt;&gt; Okay so, let me add on this
flavorful from BIDU experience.

00:35:36.830 --> 00:35:44.662
I think a data driven company means
that everything, every result,

00:35:44.662 --> 00:35:51.000
every project established, and
also even every critical question asked.

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:54.550
There would be a follow up question
from your peer or your boss,

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:57.370
like what's the data to support it.

00:35:58.710 --> 00:36:02.290
Because BIDU has 20,000 people, and

00:36:02.290 --> 00:36:06.260
every day there will be
initiatives from the bottom up.

00:36:06.260 --> 00:36:09.870
There will be command from the top and up.

00:36:09.870 --> 00:36:16.996
When Robin asked me a question, I would
say yes or no before a natural question.

00:36:16.996 --> 00:36:21.450
A natural follow-up is what's
the data to support your opinion.

00:36:21.450 --> 00:36:27.560
And that's the attitude I think
from top to the bottom that we,

00:36:27.560 --> 00:36:31.760
every engineer, every product manager
should hold to and even the sales person.

00:36:31.760 --> 00:36:33.580
because when you make a request

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:39.660
because everybody in this company gets
a lot of requests, like Simon said.

00:36:39.660 --> 00:36:44.630
And to make it a priority right, to make
the decision right, you need the data.

00:36:44.630 --> 00:36:48.420
And I think nothing comes free,
so we really need

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:53.530
the data to see whether it's
fruitful to go down this path.

00:36:53.530 --> 00:36:57.050
Obviously some people have
more intuition than others, so

00:36:57.050 --> 00:37:01.980
chances are you just need a little bit
of key data to support your opinion.

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:07.810
But still I think that is the key
attitude that you should have.

00:37:07.810 --> 00:37:11.050
That, I think, is the most important
factor as a data driven company.

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:17.402
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so actually I really
agree with what Simon said

00:37:17.402 --> 00:37:22.096
about making those data
insights happen right away.

00:37:22.096 --> 00:37:24.968
I know that one of the most annoying
things I have to do is like write a map

00:37:24.968 --> 00:37:26.005
reduce to get an answer.

00:37:26.005 --> 00:37:29.503
I write the map reduce,
four hours later it returns an answer,

00:37:29.503 --> 00:37:32.553
I've already forgotten why
I cared about the answer.

00:37:32.553 --> 00:37:38.397
But I think perhaps a more fundamental
issue really is sort of using data and

00:37:38.397 --> 00:37:42.860
having a culture that's driven
by looking at the data.

00:37:42.860 --> 00:37:49.540
Actually with your indulgence, I'd like
to make this a little interactive.

00:37:49.540 --> 00:37:54.660
A friend of mine actually works
at a teenage call center.

00:37:54.660 --> 00:37:59.430
So if you're a young teen you might be
experiencing some psychological issues or

00:37:59.430 --> 00:38:00.240
you might be depressed.

00:38:00.240 --> 00:38:05.750
You call into the hotline and they'll
have a person who talks you through it.

00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:08.030
They say one of two things when you call.

00:38:08.030 --> 00:38:12.960
Either, hi I'm a specialist,
how can I help you?

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:15.720
Or, hi I'm a counselor,
how can I help you?

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:18.500
And show of hands, I'm curious to know

00:38:18.500 --> 00:38:22.540
who in the room thinks that specialist
is better versus counselor?

00:38:22.540 --> 00:38:26.530
Everyone has to vote, so raise your
hand if you think counselor is better.

00:38:26.530 --> 00:38:27.790
Come on, raise, okay.

00:38:27.790 --> 00:38:31.700
Now, and then raise your hand if
you think specialist is better.

00:38:31.700 --> 00:38:33.920
Raise your hands, okay.

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:36.250
It looks like it's
a slightly larger group.

00:38:36.250 --> 00:38:39.400
So keep your hands up, so people who
said specialist, keep your hands up.

00:38:39.400 --> 00:38:42.180
It turns out that you're
actually in very good company.

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:46.730
They had a large meeting of experts on
this area, like experts in childhood

00:38:46.730 --> 00:38:50.980
development, and clinical development,
and psychology come together.

00:38:50.980 --> 00:38:52.450
They had two day conference and

00:38:52.450 --> 00:38:57.020
discussed this very question, and
the experts all came back and said.

00:38:57.020 --> 00:38:58.770
We need to use specialists, and

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:03.030
that was like the consensus
amongst these hotlines, for years.

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:06.450
Now okay, everyone who said counselor,
can you raise your hand?

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:10.649
Okay, so it turns out that you're not in
good company, but you're actually right.

00:39:10.649 --> 00:39:12.160
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because see,

00:39:12.160 --> 00:39:16.850
my friend who works for this hotline,
he's actually a data scientist.

00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:21.780
And he just doesn't accept what
people say, he tests it out.

00:39:21.780 --> 00:39:25.610
So he gets thousands of calls a day,
he runs it through a AD test, right?

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:29.280
A randomized control trial, and
some people get counselors,

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:30.730
some people get specialists.

00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:35.073
It turns out counselor, the rate of
people who don't hang up, who keep on

00:39:35.073 --> 00:39:40.520
having a conversation with the person
at the other end went up three-fold.

00:39:40.520 --> 00:39:46.280
So huge improvement, what are the morals
I take away from this story?

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:49.820
One, you can't always rely on experts.

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:53.080
We know from the literature,
as I'm sure you're aware,

00:39:53.080 --> 00:39:56.490
that people are really bad at
reasoning about statistics and

00:39:56.490 --> 00:39:58.630
environments where there's
a lot of randomness.

00:39:58.630 --> 00:40:01.058
We're just like biologically
not equipped to do this.

00:40:01.058 --> 00:40:03.918
That's why b,
that's why we invented statistics.

00:40:03.918 --> 00:40:07.488
And that's why we collect data and
we try to measure this stuff.

00:40:07.488 --> 00:40:09.721
But c, and I think this is the big
take away from this whole thing.

00:40:09.721 --> 00:40:13.720
The reason why my friend was allowed
to do this is because he worked at

00:40:13.720 --> 00:40:17.046
a hotline where the boss
actually believed in data.

00:40:17.046 --> 00:40:19.746
And he didn't just listen to what
the conventional wisdom was or

00:40:19.746 --> 00:40:20.750
what the experts said.

00:40:20.750 --> 00:40:23.671
He actually said lets test this out,
we can test it.

00:40:23.671 --> 00:40:27.580
And if the answer doesn't agree what
the expert said, we're changing it.

00:40:27.580 --> 00:40:31.240
I think that that kind of data driven
culture has to come from the top.

00:40:31.240 --> 00:40:36.600
Has come from what the executives say,
and if you're a leader

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:40.850
you have to hold people in our entire
organization accountable to the data.

00:40:40.850 --> 00:40:43.876
And not just we're just going to
do what everyone else is doing, or

00:40:43.876 --> 00:40:45.795
what the experts say we should be doing.

00:40:45.795 --> 00:40:47.397
&gt;&gt; Excellent.

00:40:47.397 --> 00:40:50.174
So let me close out this
part of the discussion and

00:40:50.174 --> 00:40:53.853
I'm sure in the interest of time
I'll take the final word on it.

00:40:53.853 --> 00:40:56.945
But I'm sure all the panelists
have their own anecdotes.

00:40:56.945 --> 00:41:02.115
So one of the things that those of us who
are huge fans of data-driven cultures

00:41:02.115 --> 00:41:04.185
love to tell these stories and
champion them.

00:41:04.185 --> 00:41:09.575
But we've also seen the pitfalls that can
arise when you become too data driven.

00:41:09.575 --> 00:41:13.880
And so just for example,
if you're in a search engine

00:41:13.880 --> 00:41:17.384
pretty much every change you make
is going to go to an A/B test.

00:41:17.384 --> 00:41:21.414
That is you might get 1% of users who are
going to be exposed to the new algorithm

00:41:21.414 --> 00:41:23.964
or the new font or
the new color or the new layout.

00:41:23.964 --> 00:41:26.900
And that's going to be
compared to a control group.

00:41:26.900 --> 00:41:30.960
And because that data is so strong and
so powerful and so clear and so

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:35.520
black and white, it's really easy
to pay a lot of attention to that.

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:38.740
But then, when you do something new or

00:41:38.740 --> 00:41:42.030
different or something that
might have a long-term effect.

00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:45.110
It's not necessarily going to show
up even in the right direction

00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:46.618
in a short term test.

00:41:46.618 --> 00:41:49.260
But long term tests
are much more expensive,

00:41:49.260 --> 00:41:54.930
if you're doing an advertiser experiment,
it might be disruptive for advertisers.

00:41:54.930 --> 00:41:58.520
It might change their prices or
their click volume and so on.

00:41:58.520 --> 00:42:01.072
So you don't do those long
term experiments all the time.

00:42:01.072 --> 00:42:05.458
And you have to rely on some intuition and
judgement to know,

00:42:05.458 --> 00:42:10.500
when is it worth while to really
put a change to greater scrutiny.

00:42:10.500 --> 00:42:14.480
And because you don't always do it,
you can sometimes see an organization

00:42:14.480 --> 00:42:17.990
push towards those things,
which create short term results.

00:42:17.990 --> 00:42:20.150
But might even be bad, in the long term.

00:42:20.150 --> 00:42:24.240
Just as an example,
when Bing launched its re-brand.

00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:28.420
The initial response of the users, in the
short term tests, was that they hated it.

00:42:28.420 --> 00:42:31.360
They missed their ten blue links,
they didn't understand this new,

00:42:31.360 --> 00:42:32.470
more complex interface.

00:42:32.470 --> 00:42:35.650
Search engines weren't supposed to have,
richer content,

00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:39.150
and more options to navigate the search.

00:42:40.530 --> 00:42:42.170
But it took weeks for

00:42:42.170 --> 00:42:46.330
the results to come back where the users
after a while really liked it better.

00:42:46.330 --> 00:42:49.460
So I can see that as we go forward and
firms become more and

00:42:49.460 --> 00:42:53.660
more data-driven, I think you also
expect to see firms sometimes just

00:42:53.660 --> 00:42:56.700
drive off a cliff like
sort of little by little.

00:42:56.700 --> 00:42:57.930
And then one day they wake up.

00:42:57.930 --> 00:43:00.700
The ads in the Facebook feed,
they looked fine.

00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:03.110
The users didn't mind them, they didn't
mind them, they didn't mind them.

00:43:03.110 --> 00:43:07.040
And then one day they wake up and
they've gone somewhere else.

00:43:07.040 --> 00:43:10.650
And that's something that's
a real challenge to balance.

00:43:10.650 --> 00:43:12.560
And I know everybody's
nodding their heads.

00:43:12.560 --> 00:43:13.620
They all have their own stories.

00:43:13.620 --> 00:43:17.330
But rather than hear them, what I want to
do is take the last part of the panel, and

00:43:17.330 --> 00:43:20.340
open things up to the audience for
questions.

00:43:20.340 --> 00:43:24.780
We have microphones around the room,
please come on up if you have a question.

00:43:24.780 --> 00:43:29.140
And please identify yourself and
speak from the microphone.

00:43:29.140 --> 00:43:30.840
So it's looks like we have
a question back here.

00:43:31.990 --> 00:43:35.450
&gt;&gt; Yeah, my name is Aldus I'm
with Rag Dog Industries.

00:43:35.450 --> 00:43:39.070
About 15 to 20 years ago I
remember reading lots of articles,

00:43:39.070 --> 00:43:42.000
they almost all mentioned beer and
Huggies.

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:46.600
And I think that was the first time
big data kind of came into the popular

00:43:46.600 --> 00:43:49.466
consciousness of the technology people.

00:43:49.466 --> 00:43:53.703
And it was kind of a big disaster I
recall, because we did have data but

00:43:53.703 --> 00:43:58.240
we didn't know how to ask
the questions to get the answers.

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:00.520
I'm not really sure that I'm
convinced we've learned that yet.

00:44:00.520 --> 00:44:03.070
I'd love to hear from the panel
what you guys think we have

00:44:03.070 --> 00:44:05.690
figured out in the last ten years
that will make it work this time.

00:44:08.530 --> 00:44:11.798
&gt;&gt; I think the classic example you gave,

00:44:11.798 --> 00:44:15.790
I have a confidence we
saw that to that extent.

00:44:15.790 --> 00:44:20.482
But going beyond that actually there
will be more variables in scale

00:44:20.482 --> 00:44:23.950
come out from just
a correlation of two products.

00:44:23.950 --> 00:44:26.960
Where why people buy them together.

00:44:28.030 --> 00:44:30.835
And I think that, to me,
it will solve the problem, but

00:44:30.835 --> 00:44:32.588
this again is just the beginning.

00:44:32.588 --> 00:44:34.747
And there's a lot of problems
haven't been solved.

00:44:34.747 --> 00:44:39.031
And I believe our technology will lead
to us, it's just haven't been there yet.

00:44:39.031 --> 00:44:41.877
&gt;&gt; So just from an educator's perspective,

00:44:41.877 --> 00:44:46.157
we've been talking a lot about
how we need to change education.

00:44:46.157 --> 00:44:52.489
&gt;From elementary school up through MBAs and
even PhDs in computer science or

00:44:52.489 --> 00:44:57.740
PhDs in statistics probably
don't spend enough time.

00:44:57.740 --> 00:45:02.380
Thinking conceptually about data
not just what's the algorithm and

00:45:02.380 --> 00:45:06.382
how can I improve the algorithm
to come up with a better fit.

00:45:06.382 --> 00:45:09.850
But actually, well, can I even
answer this question to start with?

00:45:09.850 --> 00:45:12.710
Even if I had a trillion observations,

00:45:12.710 --> 00:45:16.940
is the structure of the data such
that I could make a causal statement?

00:45:16.940 --> 00:45:19.170
If I changed price what would happen?

00:45:20.890 --> 00:45:24.567
And if I changed this
interface what would happen?

00:45:24.567 --> 00:45:27.537
Are you seeing more police officers
in areas with higher crime but

00:45:27.537 --> 00:45:30.460
clearly increasing police
officers doesn't increase crime.

00:45:30.460 --> 00:45:33.493
And that's in some senses,
it's obvious, and everybody knows it.

00:45:33.493 --> 00:45:37.687
But actually the expertise of really
understanding in the grey area,

00:45:37.687 --> 00:45:41.490
when can you and when can't you
learn something from the data.

00:45:41.490 --> 00:45:45.310
It's a very rear skill,
it's something that takes years to master.

00:45:45.310 --> 00:45:49.590
And it's something that I believe,
should be part of all disciplines.

00:45:49.590 --> 00:45:52.640
Reiterated over and over, and
from different directions,

00:45:52.640 --> 00:45:54.790
until people can become experts in it.

00:45:54.790 --> 00:45:56.400
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
No I think it totally requires

00:45:56.400 --> 00:45:59.077
domain knowledge, and understanding
the thing you are looking at.

00:45:59.077 --> 00:46:02.560
I mean,
one of the classic examples of this,

00:46:02.560 --> 00:46:05.450
is if you run an image search engine.

00:46:05.450 --> 00:46:10.300
If you just followed clicks as a metric
of whether or not the metric was good,

00:46:10.300 --> 00:46:14.840
you would just get pictures of scantily
clad women everywhere, no matter what.

00:46:14.840 --> 00:46:16.840
Like it doesn't matter if you Google for
Martin Luther King,

00:46:16.840 --> 00:46:18.220
it's just scantily clad pictures.

00:46:18.220 --> 00:46:20.780
And maybe temporarily it
makes the users happier, but

00:46:20.780 --> 00:46:24.650
after a while, they're going to be like,
actually I was here to do some research.

00:46:24.650 --> 00:46:26.525
I can't seem to be able to do it now.

00:46:26.525 --> 00:46:27.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So they'll go and

00:46:27.350 --> 00:46:28.910
use another search engine, right?

00:46:28.910 --> 00:46:32.240
So it's really as you say you
can sometimes follow data and

00:46:32.240 --> 00:46:33.690
drive yourself off a cliff.

00:46:33.690 --> 00:46:36.450
And you have to have that domain
knowledge and understand these things.

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:38.810
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but
I think just want to add on thing.

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:44.580
In fact, every good projects or products
actually start from a good question.

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:48.580
Like Solon just said,
can I think out this?

00:46:48.580 --> 00:46:52.300
And actually asking the right
question is the most important step.

00:46:52.300 --> 00:46:56.600
And I always feel like coming
from the engineering background,

00:46:56.600 --> 00:47:00.890
the confidence, once we know what
the right question is there will be

